Given all the tangible game-play disadvantages associated with
pacifism (difficulty of gaining experience; reliance on pet leavings
as your main food source in the early game; absolute dependence on
pets for killing everything; difficulty of surviving encounters with
things your pets won't attack or can't kill), the fact that a purely
mechanical UI issue like typographical errors can significantly
contribute to its impressiveness as an accomplishment says something,
something rather dire IMO, about the game's interface usability.

I don't mind that NetHack is hard, but it should be hard because
I have to learn and figure out how to survive, not because I have
to avoid ever accidentally pressing a key slightly too hard and
having it register twice instead of once.  It's supposed to be an
intellectually stimulating game, not a physical sport.

The more I think about it, the more I think conflating ordinary
movement with melee attacking is the real problem here, not the
passive attack of floating eyes specifically.

If your character attacks when you tell him to move because he's
confused (or stunned even), that would be different, but in the
ordinary case it seems really odd for someone just trying to walk
down a hallway to be automatically swinging a sword at anything that
happens to be there, whether you realized it was there or not.


